Kernel probe expansion
======================

kernel-probes.json inventories the 95 new source declarations, their argument
types, and locations. Linuxulator declarations expand into separate native
and compat32 providers. FIDL is the native userspace import, not a Zircon
kernel. This inventory does not assert that a running kernel exports probes.

Probe arguments are described below in arg0, arg1, ... order. Double
underscores in C declarations become hyphens in DTrace names. Empty module
and function fields should be matched with wildcards when writing scripts.
These new interfaces are diagnostic and may evolve with the underlying code.

Correlation and safety
----------------------
Pointer arguments are short-lived identities. Record the provided scalars
at the event; do not dereference an identity after an asynchronous callback,
free, unlock, or a later event. Addresses can be reused. Include PID for
userspace pointers and Linux userspace futex addresses; use taskqueue's
execution sequence for callback pairing. Sequence numbers can wrap.
No probe copies packet data, terminal characters, keys or FIDL payloads.
No new probe acquires a lock or allocates storage. Compute durations in DTrace
from paired events rather than adding clocks to normal kernel execution.
Tracing enabled under an existing lock still adds execution time.

Providers and arguments
-----------------------
taskqueue
  enqueue/coalesce: queue, task, enqueue flags / resulting pending count.
  execute-start: queue, task, execution sequence, coalesced pending count.
  execute-done: queue, task, execution sequence. The callback may free task;
    done intentionally uses only its identity.
  cancel: queue, task, result (0 or EBUSY). This is the cancellation attempt,
    not proof that a running callback has exited.
  drain-start/done: queue, task. Elapsed drain time includes sleep.
  Enqueue-to-start and start-to-done separate queue residence from execution.

bluetooth (new RFCOMM probes)
  rfcomm-state: PCB, DLCI, previous state, next state (includes initialization).
  rfcomm-close: PCB, DLCI, state at close, errno; ETIMEDOUT identifies timeout.
  rfcomm-credit: PCB, DLCI, current TX credits, current RX credits.
  rfcomm-flow: PCB, DLCI, direction (0 receive, 1 transmit), credits, modem bits.
  rfcomm-drop: session, DLCI, errno, remaining frame bytes, reason:
    1 session flow control, 2 unknown DLCI, 3 wrong DLC state,
    4 DLC flow control, 5 MTU exceeded, 6 socket receive buffer full.
    A deliberate protocol drop may have errno 0. A successful credit-only
    frame does not count as a drop.
  rfcomm-frame-error: session, errno from frame dispatch/validation.
    This can overlap a more specific drop event; do not sum both as packets.

linuxulator / linuxulator32, module epoll
  ctl-done: epoll fd, target fd, ctl operation, errno; includes rejected calls.
  translate: epoll fd, target fd, Linux event mask, number of native filters.
  deliver: target ident, native filter, translated Linux mask. This happens
    before copyout, so it is a translation event, not proof of user delivery.
  wait-start: epoll fd, maxevents. wait-done: epoll fd, event count, errno.
    Count is zero on failure. Scope is linux_epoll_wait_ts, after outer
    timeout/signal-mask conversion, not every syscall validation failure.

linuxulator / linuxulator32, module futex
  wait-start: userspace address, bitset, sharing flags.
  wait-done: address, errno (including mismatch, timeout, signal/fault).
  wake-done: address, requested wake count, actual count or zero on error, errno.
  requeue-done: source address, destination address, requested wakes,
    total woken-plus-requeued (zero on failure), errno.
  pi-done: address, operation (1 lock/trylock, 2 unlock), errno.
  owner-death: address, PI boolean, had-waiters boolean; after successful
    owner-death update, before wakeup. Existing futex_waitv probes remain.

umtx
  sleep-start: wait-queue identity, key type (sys/umtxvar.h).
  sleep-done: wait-queue identity, raw sleep result. Higher layers may translate
    this result or observe a racing wake; it is not the final syscall errno.
  wake: key identity, requested wake count, actual count.
  pi-owner: PI object, owner thread, action (1 assign, 0 disown).

ffs
  alloc-done: inode, logical block, requested bytes, allocated block, errno.
  alloc-retry: inode, requested bytes; cleanup before allocation retry.
  alloc-fallback: inode, cylinder group, size/mode, stage
    (2 quadratic rehash, 3 brute-force search). The allocator also serves inode
    allocation, so the size argument can be an inode mode.
  worklist-start: mount, full boolean, queued work count.
  worklist-done: mount, processed count, remaining queued count.
    No pair is emitted for mounts without softdep state.
  journal-start/done: mount, journal queue count (only SUJ flushes).
  slowdown: mount, journal-low boolean, inode dependencies, directory removals.
    This signals cleanup pressure; journaled mounts may accelerate cleanup
    without actually blocking the requesting thread.

bufcache
  space-wait: buffer domain, vnode, getblk flags.
  space-resume: buffer domain, msleep result. One pair per actual sleep.
  flush-start: vnode, domain, target count.
  flush-done: vnode, domain, return count from the first flush pass. The
    rollback-dependent fallback pass is not included in that return count.
  reclaim: domain, KVA-only boolean, result (0 or ENOBUFS).
  io-wait: buffer. io-done: buffer, errno from bufwait.

lockf / nlm
  lockf conflict: waiter lock, blocking lock, waiter start/end byte offsets.
  deadlock: waiter, blocker, errno from adding the dependency edge.
  grant: lock, start/end offsets at activation. Unlock operations also pass
    through activation; inspect the live lock type when interpreting this event.
  wake: pending lock removed and caller notified (also used for async NLM).
  cancel: lock, start/end offsets, before unlink/free.
  wait-start: lock, vnode, start/end offsets. wait-done: lock, sx_sleep result,
    before subsequent lock-state validation; this is not final syscall errno.
  nlm wait-start: waiting-lock handle, timeout ticks. wait-done: handle, errno.
  nlm cancel: vnode. recovery-start/done: host, monitor state. Done means the
    recovery routine returned, not that every remote lock was recovered.

tty
  read-start: tty, VMIN, VTIME (termios units); read-done: tty, errno.
    This brackets the line-discipline read, including immediate returns.
  flow: tty, stopped boolean. watermark: tty, output high-water blocked boolean.
  input-drop: tty, reason 1 (input queue cannot accept the character).
  carrier: tty, carrier-present boolean, before CLOCAL/open-state handling.

linuxulator / linuxulator32, module socket
  setopt-done/getopt-done: fd, Linux level, Linux option, errno.
  sockopt-map: native level, Linux option, native option (or unsupported value).
  cmsg-map: Linux SOL_SOCKET control type, native type (or unsupported value).
  cmsg-reject: Linux level, Linux type, direction 1 (send).
  cmsg-truncate: fd, total ancillary capacity, bytes already copied, at the
    branch setting CTRUNC. These are metadata; no ancillary payload is exposed.

linuxkpi_work / iwlwifi
  state: address of work state field, old/new WORK_ST_* enum values.
    The atomic transition has committed; a later concurrent state change may
    already have happened. Do not use probe ordering as a synchronization proof.
  execute-start: workqueue, work, pending. execute-done: workqueue, work.
    Work may free itself; completion never dereferences it.
  cancel-start/flush-start: work. done: work, boolean return value (not errno).
  iwlwifi trace:dev_irq: device.
  trace:dev_irq_msix: device, MSI-X entry identity, upstream boolean,
    FH interrupt bits, hardware interrupt bits.
  trace:dev_tx: device, skb identity, TFD bytes, header bytes, padding bytes.
    New IRQ/TX calls compile away when KDTRACE_HOOKS is disabled.

vm / mac_capability / kqueue / vfs
  vm fault-cow: fault-state identity, rename boolean (otherwise copy).
  fault-pager-start: fault state, object, page index; done: fault state,
    VM_PAGER_* result (not errno).
  fault-busy-start: fault state, allocation flags; done: fault state.
  fault-oom: fault state, stage (0 allocation-pressure retry path, 1 OOM action).
  mac_capability direct-accept: message identity, destination badge,
    resulting queue length, reply token. message-receive: message, receiver badge.
    Their difference measures accepted-direct-message queue residence.
  direct-fallback: message, peer badge, reason (1 dead, 2 no service, 3 full).
    Covers failed direct-room checks, not disabled direct mode or missing peers.
  kqueue detach-start: knote, ident/fd, filter, file identity (NULL for non-fd).
    detach-done: knote. Both surround f_detach, before knote reclamation.
  vfs detach-start/done: knote, vnode, surrounding knlist removal before vdrop.
    These can correlate the filedesc/kqueue/vnode paths with lockstat.

fidl_bsd$target (userspace)
  validation-failed: incoming identity, txid, ordinal, reason:
    1 size/count (txid zero, expected ordinal), 2 header/ordinal/txid/version,
    3 event-versus-request kind mismatch. Unvalidated IDs are peer metadata.
  reject: connection, txid, ordinal, reason
    (4 malformed epitaph, 5 closing, 6 missing handler).
  epitaph: connection, peer's signed protocol status; this is not local errno.
  transport: connection, libchannel identity, txid, ordinal.
  message-token: connection, txid, ordinal, libchannel message token. This
    connects validated received requests/replies to libchannel tracing.

Qualification
-------------
With a prepared kernel and module object tree:
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/kernel-compile.py \
    --kernel-obj /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG

This compiles 48 cases with -Werror and all logical CPUs: each changed kernel
translation unit and IRQ/TX caller, both Linuxulator ABIs, hooks on and off.
It writes objects, compiler logs, commands and source hashes under
/usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-validation. It does not install, load modules or reboot.
Use a root shell if /usr/obj permissions require it. Keep source header order;
several inherited subsystems have dependency-sensitive include sequences.

Before runtime qualification, boot a matching test kernel and load the needed
modules in a test VM. Verify actual enumeration, then exercise controlled
workloads: UFS allocation/fsync, conflicting byte-range locks, PTY flow control,
epoll readiness/timeouts, futex wake/requeue, channel direct/fallback delivery,
and VM COW/file faults. Confirm expected probe counts, paired events, no DTrace
errors/drops, and unchanged operation results. Radio/firmware events need a
real device or validated emulator. Avoid inducing host memory pressure.

The host used for this change has no DTrace device. Compile/link checks and
functional FIDL tests do not qualify live kernel or USDT firing. On a DTrace
capable test host, a root shell can run the FIDL runtime fixture under:
  dtrace -q -n 'fidl_bsd$target:::message-token { @tokens = count(); } \
    fidl_bsd$target:::validation-failed { @bad[arg3] = count(); }' \
    -c /path/to/tracing-enabled/runtime-test
Require nonzero token and malformed-message counts, not merely exit success.
